---
import Prose from './Prose.astro';
import CodeBlock from './CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from './Callout.astro';
import { ENV_VARS, ENV_BEST_PRACTICES } from '../data/guide';

const backendVars = ENV_VARS.filter((v) => v.scope === 'backend');
const frontendVars = ENV_VARS.filter((v) => v.scope === 'buyer-app');
const varList = 'mt-3 grid gap-x-6 gap-y-2 text-sm sm:grid-cols-[16rem_1fr]';
---

<Prose class="max-w-none">
  <p>
    Environment configuration is handled differently at each tier. The backend is the only workspace
    that needs real setup; the buyer and admin apps take a couple of build-time public values.
  </p>

  <h3>Development</h3>
  <p>Copy the example file and fill it in. The real .env is git-ignored:</p>
  <CodeBlock title="terminal" code={'cp backend/.env.example backend/.env'} />
  <dl class={varList}>
    {[...backendVars, ...frontendVars].map((v) => (
      <div class="contents">
        <dt class="font-mono text-fg">{v.name}</dt>
        <dd class="text-muted">{v.purpose}</dd>
      </div>
    ))}
  </dl>
  <p>MONGODB_URI can point at a local mongod or an Atlas dev cluster.</p>

  <h3>Testing / CI</h3>
  <p>
    No real secrets live in the repository. Genuine test secrets are GitHub Actions Secrets &mdash;
    TechCart's workflow reads ATLAS_SEARCH_TEST_URI that way. Values that are needed at build time
    but are not sensitive are set inline in the workflow:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    title="ci.yml"
    code={
      'env:\n  NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL: http://localhost:4000\n  NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID: dummy-client-id'
    }
  />

  <h3>Production</h3>
  <p>
    Set in the Vercel and Render dashboards, one set per environment. Each of development, staging
    and production has its own MongoDB Atlas cluster and its own Razorpay key pair, so a mistake or
    a leak in one environment cannot reach another.
  </p>

  <Callout type="warning">
    Never commit a real <code>.env</code>. Commit only <code>.env.example</code> with placeholder
    values.
  </Callout>

  <Callout type="warning">
    A <code>PUBLIC_</code> (Astro) or <code>NEXT_PUBLIC_</code> (Next.js) prefix means the value is
    bundled into the browser. Never put a secret behind one.
  </Callout>

  <h3>Best practices</h3>
  <ul>
    {ENV_BEST_PRACTICES.map((practice) => (
      <li>{practice}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>
</Prose>
